<GuideLayout
  title="Navigating GRS Certification for Sustainable Activewear"
  type="SUSTAINABILITY"
  description="What activewear brands should know about GRS certification, recycled fabric claims, supplier documentation and responsible sourcing workflows."
>
  <p>GRS certification helps brands communicate recycled material claims with more confidence. For activewear, it is most relevant when using recycled nylon, recycled polyester or other recycled textile inputs in leggings, sports bras, tops and sets.</p>

  <h2>What GRS Helps Verify</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Recycled material input and chain-of-custody documentation.</li>
    <li>Supplier-level traceability for selected certified materials.</li>
    <li>Basic social and environmental processing requirements.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What GRS Does Not Replace</h2>
  <p>GRS does not automatically prove that a finished legging is squat-proof, durable or suitable for premium retail. Brands still need garment-level testing for fit, stretch recovery, colorfastness, shrinkage and pilling.</p>

  <h2>Fitclo Buyer Advice</h2>
  <p>Ask for fabric certificates, composition sheets and production documentation before using recycled claims in marketing. Keep claim language precise so your product page does not overstate what the certification covers.</p>
</GuideLayout>
